Claws Mail @VERSION@ - an email client (GTK 3) for RISC OS

Claws Mail 4.4.0 by the Claws Mail team (GPL version 3), on GTK 3 through
riscos-gtk's RISC OS backend. Secure connections (TLS) go through RISC
OS's own AcornSSL module.

POP3 and IMAP to fetch mail, SMTP to send it; mail kept in local (MH)
folders or on the IMAP server. HTML mail is shown laid out, with its
pictures (Claws Mail's LiteHTML viewer, built in). Spelling is checked
as you type (British English; more dictionaries in
<Choices$Write>.GTK.Dictionaries, as for every GTK program). RSS feeds,
Sieve filters on your mail server, and a calendar (vCalendar) are
plug-ins, built in: see below.

Attachments
- Double-click an attachment (or choose Open) to open it in the program
  the desktop has for it, as a double-click in a Filer window would. A
  file without a RISC OS type is given the one its MIME type (or name)
  has in MimeMap first.
- Drag an attachment to a Filer window to save it there.
- SVG pictures are shown.

Help > Manual opens Claws Mail's manual (HTML, in the application) in
your browser.

Needs
- RISC OS 5.28 or later (for AcornSSL), in a 16 or 32 bpp screen mode.
- SharedUnixLibrary, ARMEABISupport and Iconv (PackMan).
- An internet connection set up in !Configure (Network, with a name
  server for the Resolver).

Starting
- Run it: Claws Mail puts its icon on the icon bar. Click Select on the
  icon to open its main window. The first time, it asks for your name,
  email address and your mail servers (POP3 or IMAP, and SMTP), and where
  to keep your mail, then opens the main window.
- Closing the main window leaves Claws Mail on the icon bar, still
  checking mail; Select on the icon opens it again. Quit is on the icon
  bar menu (and File > Exit).
- Use "SSL/TLS" (pop3s port 995, imaps port 993, smtps port 465) or
  "STARTTLS" for your servers where your provider supports them: almost
  all do now. All of it goes through AcornSSL.
- IMAP: your server's folders appear in the folder list; opening a
  message fetches it (copies are kept in <Choices$Write>.ClawsMail's
  /claws-mail.imapcache). IMAP works in a thread of its own, so the
  desktop keeps going while it talks to the server.
- The icon bar icon: Select shows the main window; Menu gives Info (About
  this program), Choices... (Claws Mail's Preferences) and Quit.
- Drop a file on the icon (or on !ClawsMail in a Filer window) to start a
  new message with the file attached. Its RISC OS file type gives its
  type (a PDF is sent as application/pdf) and, if its name has no
  extension, one is added (Report becomes Report.pdf) for other systems.
- Quit (or a desktop shutdown) keeps any messages you were writing in
  Drafts, and asks if unsent mail is still in the Queue.

Where things are
- Settings: <Choices$Write>.ClawsMail (the /claws-mail directory there).
- Your mail: <Choices$Write>.ClawsMail.Mail, unless you chose another
  place when Claws Mail first started. Each message is a file.
- Messages go to <Wimp$ScrapDir>.ClawsMail.Log. Please send it, with a
  note of anything that goes wrong.

Using it
- Click Menu over a window for its menu (File, Edit, View, Message...).
  Keyboard short-cuts are shown in the menus.
- Get Mail fetches mail for your accounts; Write starts a new message;
  Send in the message window sends it.
- The server's certificate is checked by AcornSSL; Claws Mail's own
  certificate manager isn't available.

HTML mail
- An HTML message is shown as its sender laid it out: fonts, colours,
  tables and the pictures sent with it. When a message has both a plain
  text and an HTML version, the HTML one is shown (Choices... > Message
  View > Text Options > "Select the HTML part of multipart/alternative
  messages" to change that). The list of parts on the right of the
  message switches between them.
- Pictures on the web (remote content) aren't fetched unless you ask:
  senders use them to see when you read a message. To load them, turn
  on "Enable loading of remote content" in Choices... > Plugins >
  LiteHTML. They are fetched over http or https, through AcornSSL.
- The message's headers (From, To, Subject, Date) are shown above it,
  as above a plain text message.
- Menu over the message gives the window's menu; over a link, its
  first entry is "Link >" (Open Link, Copy Link).

Emoji
- Emoji in messages are shown in colour, at the size of the text, once
  a colour emoji font is installed for FontConfig: install PackMan's
  FontConfig and riscos-gtk's TTF-Noto-Color-Emoji package (on the
  riscos-gtk release page and in PackMan). Without it, emoji show as
  boxes with their code in, or as plain black shapes.
- To put one in a message you're writing: Message > Insert emoji... in
  the message window (or Ctrl-.). A chooser opens at the caret: pick a
  group from the row of icons along its foot, or type part of a name in
  its search field, and click the emoji. It goes into the field you were
  typing in: the message, or the Subject line. Recently used emoji are
  kept at the start while ClawsMail runs.

Links
- Clicking a link (in HTML or plain text mail) sends it to your web
  browser through RISC OS's URI handler. If neither Iris nor NetSurf is
  running, ClawsMail starts Iris (or NetSurf if the Filer hasn't seen
  Iris) and hands it the link once it's running. A browser that's
  already running takes the link.
- A mailto: link starts a new message in ClawsMail.

Icons
- The toolbar, folder list and message list use RISC OS icons. The
  folders are the Filer's own directory icons from your desktop theme,
  with a small sign for Inbox, Sent, Queue and Drafts. Claws Mail's own
  icons can be chosen in Choices... > Display > Themes.

Not yet
- News (NNTP) is built in but untested; other plug-ins; address books
  other than Claws Mail's own.

Built with riscos-crossdev 1.3 and UnixLib 5.0.3.3.

